From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 07:56:59 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] VerityUtils: treat a kernel without fs-verity as unsupported,
 not failed

setUpFsverity throws on any non-zero errno. A kernel built without
CONFIG_FS_VERITY answers FS_IOC_ENABLE_VERITY with ENOTTY, so on this
device every PackageManager state write throws and is logged with a
full stack trace:

  Failed to enable fs-verity on FD(2486): Inappropriate ioctl for device

That is ~550 error lines a boot between ResilientAtomicFile and
AccessPersistence, which is enough noise to hide the failures worth
reading. The method's own contract is "enables fs-verity, if supported
by the filesystem", and ENOTTY/EOPNOTSUPP is exactly the filesystem
saying it is not. Return quietly; nothing is lost that the kernel could
have provided. Any other errno still throws.
---
 core/java/com/android/internal/security/VerityUtils.java | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/core/java/com/android/internal/security/VerityUtils.java b/core/java/com/android/internal/security/VerityUtils.java
index ac186d0a26b5..9e7f855476da 100644
--- a/core/java/com/android/internal/security/VerityUtils.java
+++ b/core/java/com/android/internal/security/VerityUtils.java
@@ -71,6 +71,14 @@ public abstract class VerityUtils {
     /** Enables fs-verity for an open file without signature. */
     public static void setUpFsverity(int fd) throws IOException {
         int errno = enableFsverityForFdNative(fd);
+        // "Enables fs-verity, if supported by the filesystem" is the contract, and a kernel without
+        // CONFIG_FS_VERITY answers the ioctl with ENOTTY. Treating that as a failure means every
+        // PackageManager state write throws and is logged with a full stack: ~550 error lines a boot
+        // from ResilientAtomicFile and AccessPersistence alone, which buries the failures that
+        // matter. Nothing is lost that the kernel could have provided.
+        if (errno == OsConstants.ENOTTY || errno == OsConstants.EOPNOTSUPP) {
+            return;
+        }
         if (errno != 0) {
             throw new IOException("Failed to enable fs-verity on FD(" + fd + "): "
                     + Os.strerror(errno));
